        "source": "claude",
        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz remains at the center of a live military and commercial disruption. The most operationally significant development in the past six hours is a gCaptain report, attributed to three people familiar with the data and not independently confirmed, that the United States has consumed virtually all of its long-range precision missile inventory over five months of conflict with Iran. That report, if accurate, carries material implications for force-projection assumptions that shipping and insurance desks are beginning to factor in. Physical throughput continues to reflect the severity of the crisis: PortWatch recorded 10 transits on 2026-07-23, its most recently published day, against a pre-crisis baseline of 88 per day. Separately, our own tracking shows port arrivals across the Hormuz-area ports reached 650 vessels in the most recent 24 hours, a figure drawn from a different methodology covering five ports on both the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman sides and not comparable to the PortWatch transit count. Brent stood at $80.36 at 14:52 UTC, down 3.71% over the preceding 24 hours. The Hormuz Index Crisis Pressure composite sits in the extreme band, easing over 24 hours, with physical transit deviation as the leading driver; the Escalation Forecast registers in the elevated band, also easing, led by Polymarket escalation contract pricing. The two composites diverge in severity. On diplomacy, Qatar's foreign ministry spokesperson said Doha remains hopeful a US-Iran agreement could be reached within hours or days, per Mehr News, Iranian state media; that claim has not been confirmed by independent sources. CNN reported Trump held off further Iran strikes, subject to a deal being agreed, a conditional posture, not a settled end to hostilities.",
